Here is the procedure for installing Gamma and the Debugger from the
source distribution on GNU/Linux.

You will need [CMake](https://cmake.org/) to configure and build the
source code. Use your system's package manager to install it, then
unpack Gamma's distribution archive and prepare the build directory.

```bash
$ tar -zxf gamma-GAMMA_VERSION.tar.gz
$ mkdir gamma-GAMMA_VERSION/build
$ cd gamma-GAMMA_VERSION/build
```

## Preparing to Build Gamma

Gamma requires a version of the [GCC compiler](https://gcc.gnu.org/)
that supports the C++17 language standard. In practice, this means
that any relatively modern version should do.  You will additionally
need the following libraries, which you can install through your
system's package manager:

  * [GMP](https://gmplib.org/)
  * [MPFR](https://www.mpfr.org/)
  * [Boost](https://www.boost.org/)
  * [zlib](https://zlib.net/)
  * [Eigen](https://eigen.tuxfamily.org/)

You also need [CGAL](https://github.com/CGAL/cgal) version 6.1.2.  The
easiest way to get it, is to download the source distribution archive
from the
[Releases](https://github.com/CGAL/cgal/releases#release-v6.1.2) page
of the CGAL project.  Unpack it inside the build directory.

```bash
$ wget https://github.com/CGAL/cgal/releases/download/v6.1.2/\
CGAL-6.1.2.tar.xz
$ tar -Jxf CGAL-6.1.2.tar.xz
```

Depending on the language front ends you want to enable, you will also
need [GNU Guile](https://www.gnu.org/software/guile/guile.html) and
[Lua](https://www.lua.org).  These are optional and if one is not
available, the respective language front end will be disabled.

CMake tries to find Guile using the c`pkg-config` tool.  Your
GNU/Linux distribution should include a f`.pc` file in Guile's
development package, and CMake should find it once you install it.
Alternatively, Guile can be compiled from its source distribution,
which includes and installs a f`.pc` file.

For example:

```bash
$ wget https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/guile/guile-3.0.11.tar.gz
$ tar -zxf guile-3.0.11.tar.gz
$ cd guile-3.0.11
$ ./configure
$ make -j8
$ sudo make install
$ cd ..
```

If you install Lua from your system's package manager, CMake will find
it, but may try to link against a version of the Lua library compiled
for use by C programs.  If your distribution includes a C++ version of
the library, you can tell CMake where to find it by setting the
`LUA_LIBRARY` variable.  For example, Debian includes such a version
of the library and CMake can be directed to use it by adding an option
such as `-DLUA_LIBRARY=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/liblua5.4-c++.so` to
the CMake invocation shown in ref: Building the Programs.

Alternatively, you can build Lua from its source distribution as follows:

```bash
$ wget https://www.lua.org/ftp/lua-5.4.8.tar.gz
$ tar -zxf lua-5.4.8.tar.gz
$ cd lua-5.4.8/src/
$ make CC=g++ liblua.a
$ cd ../..
```

In this case, you will have to set the `Lua_ROOT` variable when
invocing CMake, as is shown in ref: Building the Programs.

## Preparing to Build Gamma Debugger

The Debugger requires a version of [GCC](https://gcc.gnu.org/) with
support for the C23 language standard.  In practice this means version
13 and later.  You will also need the following libraries, which you
can install through your system's package manager:

  * OpenGL, typically provided by the [Mesa 3D Graphics
    Library](https://mesa3d.org/)
  * [GFLW](https://www.glfw.org/) version 3
  * [Freetype](https://freetype.org/)
  * [Fontconfig](https://www.freedesktop.org/wiki/Software/fontconfig/)

If you want to be able to create high quality figures with the `print`
command, you will additionally need the
[GL2PS](https://www.geuz.org/gl2ps/) library.  If it is not found, the
command will not be available.  You will also not be able to build the
documentation, as its figures are built using the `print` command.

## Building the Programs

You can now build and install both Gamma and the Debugger with:

```bash
$ cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCGAL_ROOT=./CGAL-6.1.2 \
    -DLua_ROOT=./lua-5.4.8/src ..
$ make -j4
$ sudo make install
```

You can substitute a higher or lower number in s`-j4` above, according
to the number of CPU cores and memory available on your machine.  To
build either Gamma or the Debugger on their own, use s`make gamma`, or
s`make gammadb` respectively.  Type s`make help` for a list of
available targets.

You can then install Gamma without the Debugger with s`sudo cmake
--install . --component gamma`.  Substitute s`gammadb` for s`gamma` if
you want to install the just the Debugger.  CMake will install in
f`/usr/local` by default; consult its documentation if you'd like to
change this, or other aspects of the build.

## Building the Documentation

There are mutliple editions of the reference manual, one for each
supported language and each is available in HTML and PDF form.
Furthermore, there is also a manual for Gamma's source code itself, in
the manner of literate programming.

The documentation is in part extracted from comments in the source
code.  The rest exists as a set of dedicated documentation input
files.  It is written using a markup language and you will need [GNU
Awk](https://www.gnu.org/software/gawk/) to assemble and prepare it
for typesetting and [GNU
Texinfo](https://www.gnu.org/software/texinfo/) to process it into
either HTML or PDF form.

Depending on the documentation you want to build, you may also need
one or more of the following:

  * One or more of the [Graphviz](https://graphviz.org/) graph
    visualization programs.  These are mostly used in the source code
    manual.

  * A @LaTeX{} distribution if you want to build documentation in PDF
    form.  You will need the `xcolor` and `listings` @LaTeX{} packages.
    You may also need `tkz-euclide`, depending on the manual you want
    to build.

When building HTML documentation:

  * [GNU Source-highlight](https://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite/)
    is used to highlight code listings.

  * [GNU Ghostscript](https://www.gnu.org/software/ghostscript/)
    converts the PDF figures produced by the c`print` command to
    raster images.

CMake will only check for the Texinfo and Awk programs and disable the
documentation targets if it doesn't find them.  Otherwise, the
following make targets should be available to build documentation.

  weave-lua-html := Build the Lua edition of the reference manual in
  HTML form.

  weave-lua-pdf := Build the Lua edition of the reference manual in
  PDF form.

  weave-scheme-html := Build the Scheme edition of the reference
  manual in HTML form.

  weave-scheme-pdf := Build the Scheme edition of the reference manual
  in PDF form.

  weave-program-pdf := Build the source code manual.  This is only
  available in PDF form.

Built documentation is placed inside the f`doc/` subdirectory of your
build directory, either as f`.pdf` files, or as subdirectories
containing f`.html` files.
